From 171150f1e98f350503da1f68cb227baf8099b251 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ayiejosh <132535860+ayiejosh@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 09:28:51 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 262/269] gallium/pvr: compute fallback draw consumes the
 pipeline's vertex buffer and any RGBA8 target size

The fallback is now a real pipeline draw instead of a fixed triangle:
- the rasteriser shader takes its three clip-space vertices and the target size from a parameter storage
  buffer, so draw_vbo's vertex-buffer contents and the bound render-target dimensions drive what is drawn;
- pvr_context's draw_vbo collects the vertices from the bound vertex buffer (vec3 positions, one per
  vertex, w = 1 as the fallback's vertex stage writes it) and falls back to the original window-space
  triangle when none is bound;
- the descriptor set now carries two buffers (binding 0 = render target, binding 1 = draw parameters),
  generated from the same pco data; the CDM control stream is rebuilt when the dispatch shape changes, so
  the fallback works for any RGBA8 2D target whose dimensions are multiples of the 8x8 local size;
- the compute state records the PDS program geometry and binding offsets so streams are regenerated
  correctly rather than tied to the first draw.

Probe (PVR_PROBE_COMPUTE_DRAW=1) now covers two cases through the pipe_context API:
  512x512, no vertex buffer   -> inside=73728 (the reference coverage, exact), centre=0xFF0000FF
  256x256, full-screen vertex-buffer triangle -> inside=65536/65536 (every pixel)
Both PASS, 2 draws executed, and the probe prints --- ALL PASS with and without the env var and under the
drm-shim. (pco crashes in pco_cf when the shader has an early `return` before a store, so the shader avoids
one; the fallback therefore requires target dimensions that are multiples of the local size.)
